Define the  GLOBAL indices of the wavevector q as (nx,ny,nz), i.e.

    q = (2*pi*nx/Lx,2*pi*ny/Ly,2*pi*nz/Lz)

with Lx, Ly, and Lz being the system sizes in real space. If the
array is arr(nx,ny,nz) then the correct symmetry is

   conjugate(array(nx,ny,nz)) = array(kx*Nx-nx,ky*Ny-ny,kz*Nz-nz)

where Nx, Ny, Nz are the grid point numbers, and kx, ky, and kz
are all (arbitrary) integers. The relevant case for us is

   conjugate(array(nx,ny,nz)) = array(Nx-nx,Ny-ny,Nz-nz).


First, we note that the box in Fourier space chosen by DFT is
not the first Brillouin zone. Below, we show for Nx = 4,
Ny = 10, and Nz = 24 the Fourier space domain used in DFT.


    Nx//2 ________________________
         /                       /|
        /                       / | 
    0  /______________________ /  |
  Ny-1 |                       |  |
       |                       |  |
       |                       |  |
       |                       |  |
       |                       |  |
       |                       |  |
       |                       |  /
       |                       | /
       |_______________________|/
     0
       0                      Nz-1


We need to rearrane this into the Brillouin zone so first,
we divide it into blocks


    Nx//2 ________________________
         /            /          /|
        /            /          / | 
    0  /____________/__________/  |
 Ny-1  |            |          |  |
       |    ^       |   ^      |  /
       |    C->     |   D->    | /|
       |____________|__________|/ |
 Ny//2 |            |          |  |
       |    ^       |   ^      |  |
       |    A ->    |   B->    |  /
       |            |          | /
       |____________|__________|/
     0
       0          Nz//2       Nz-1


Due to periodicity, we know that

    array(nx,ny,nz) ->  array(nx+kx*Nx,ny+ky*Ny,nz+kz*Nz)

where Nx, Ny, Nz are the grid point numbers, and kx, ky, and kz
are all (arbitrary) integers.
    
Block A stays where it is. The transform applied is
the identity,

    array(nx,ny,nz) ->  array(nx,ny,nz)

and the vertices stay the same at

(i,0,0)
(i,Ny//2,0)
(i,0,Nz//2)
(i,Ny//2,Nz//2)

(i,0,0)
(i,Ny//2,0)
(i,0,Nz//2)
(i,Ny//2,Nz//2)

for i in {0,Nx//2}.

Block B has to transform. The transform applied is

  array(nx,ny,nz) ->  array(nx,ny,nz-Nz)

so the vertices are moved to

(i,0,Nz//2+1)     -> (0,0,1-Nz//2)
(i,0,Nz-1)        -> (0,0,-1)
(i,Ny//2,Nz//2+1) -> (0,Ny//2,1-Nz//2)
(i,Ny//2,Nz-1)    -> (0,Ny//2,-1)

for i in {0,Nx//2}.

Block C has to transform. The transform applied is

  array(nx,ny,nz) ->  array(nx,ny-Ny,nz)

so the vertices are moved to

(i,Ny//2+1,0)     -> (0,1-Ny//2,0)
(i,Ny-1,0)        -> (0,-1,0)
(i,Ny//2+1,Nz//2) -> (0,1-Ny//2,Nz//2)
(i,Ny-1,Nz//2)    -> (0,-1,Nz//2)

for i in {0,Nx//2}.

Block D has to transform. The transform applied is

  array(nx,ny,nz) ->  array(nx,ny-Ny,nz-Nz)

so the vertices are moved to

(i,Ny//2+1,Nz//2+1) -> (0,1-Ny//2,1-Nz//2)
(i,Ny-1,Nz//2+1)    -> (0,-1,1-Nz//2)
(i,Ny//2+1,Nz-1)    -> (0,1-Ny//2,-1)
(i,Ny-1,Nz-1)       -> (0,-1,-1)

for i in {0,Nx//2}.


Therefore, the Brillouin zone is

    Nx//2 ________________________
         /          /            /|
        /          /            / | 
    0  /__________/____________/  |
 Ny//2 |          |            |  |
       |      ^   |    ^       |  |
       |    <-B   |    A->     |  /
       |          |            | /|
       |__________|____________|/ |
    0  |          |            |  |
       |   <-D    |    C->     |  /
       |     ⌄    |    ⌄       | / 
       |__________|____________|/
1-Ny//2       
      1-Nz//2     0          Nz//2



Practically, the way to approach this when calling a
function which depends on qx,qy,qz is to do


for (int kz = 0; kz < ft_phi.Nz(); kz ++ ) {
  if (kz + ft_phi.get_local0start() > ft_phi.global_Nz()/2)
    qz = (-ft_phi.global_Nz() + kz + ft_phi.get_local0start() ) * dqz;
  else
    qz = ( kz + ft_phi.get_local0start() ) * dqz;
  for (int jy = 0; jy < ft_phi.Ny(); jy ++ ) {
    if (jy > ft_phi.global_Ny()/2)
      qz = (-ft_phi.global_Ny() + jy ) * dqy;
    else
      qy = jy * dqy;
    for (int ix = 0; ix < ft_phi.Nx(); ix ++ ) {
      qx = ix * dqx;
      ft_phi(ix,jy,kz) = PHI(qx,qy,qz);
      // PHI(qx,qy,qz) is some function like sin(qx)*4*qy + qz
    }
  }
}



